Let $x$ be the price of the house. Then 80% of $x$ plus $49,400$ is equal to the price $x$ of the house. We just have to solve the equation $0.8x+49,400=x$.</think>
Think: The problem describes 2 types of payment for the house: a loan and savings. The loan was 80% of the total of the price, the savings was 20% of the price. We can then say
\begin{equation*}
L+S = Price
\end{equation*}
where $L$ is the loan, and $S$ is the savings, so
\begin{equation*}
0.8x +49,400 = x
\end{equation*}
This is an equation we want to solve! Let's do the algebra:
\begin{eqnarray*}
0.8x&=&x-49,400\\
0.8x-x&=&-49,400\\
0.2x&=&49,400
\end{eqnarray*}
So the price of the house is
\begin{equation*}
 x = 49,400/0.2 = 247,000.
\end{equation*}
<answer>Answer: $247,000</answer>

 Here we have $n=2$ logarithmic terms with different bases. It seems we should start by using the change of base: \begin{align*}
\log_ i a &= \frac{\log_2 a}{\log_2 i}
\end{align*} Using this formula, we can rewrite the equation as \begin{align*}
\frac{\log_2 2018}{\log_2 2} + \frac{\log_2 2018}{\log_2 4} + \frac{\log_2 2018}{\log_2 8} + \frac{\log_2 2018}{\log_2 64} &= \log_x 2018.
\end{align*} Note that all the denominators can easily be transformed into powers of $2$. \begin{align*}
\frac{\log_2 2018}{1} + \frac{\log_2 2018}{2} + \frac{\log_2 2018}{3} + \frac{\log_2 2018}{6} &= \log_x 2018.
\end{align*} Pulling out a $\log_2 2018$ will yield
\begin{align*}
(\log_2 2018) \left (1 + \frac{1}{2} + \frac{1}{3} + \frac{1}{6} \right ) &= \log_x 2018.
\end{align*} Completing the fraction addition, we get \begin{align*}
(\log_2 2018) \left (\frac{12}{12} + \frac{6}{12} + \frac{4}{12} + \frac{2}{12} \right ) &= \log_x 2018 \\
(\log_2 2018) \left ( \frac{24}{12} \right ) &= \log_x 2018 \\
(\log_2 2018) \left ( 2 \right ) &= \log_x 2018 \\
2 \log_2 2018 &= \log_x 2018.
\end{align*} The logarithm with base $n$ can be pulled out. \begin{align*}
\log_2 (2018^2) &= \log_x 2018.
\end{align*} Completing the exponent addition, we get \begin{align*}
\log_2 4076324 &= \log_x 2018.
\end{align*} Since logarithms can only equal one another when they have equal bases (and are evaluated using the same base), we get $2 = x$, i.e., \[x = \boxed{2}.\]
