 Here we have $n=6$ cards, $n_b = 3$ cards where both sides are black, $1$ black-red card, and $n_r= i= 2$ cards where both sides are red. The probability of seeing a black side is $\frac{(3+1\cdot0) \cdot 1/2}{6\cdot1/2}$, so by complementarity we can see
\begin{align*}
p = \frac{i/2}{(i+1).(1/2)+(3+1\cdot0) \cdot 1/2 } = \frac{1/2}{2/2+3/2} = \frac 1 5.
\end{align*}
Now multipling this probability by $p$ we get the probability of seeing a red card to be
\begin{align*}
\frac{i}{n } = \frac 2 6 = \frac 1 3.
\end{align*} 
And now the probability of seeing a red card which is also red on the other side is $2/3$ times the probability of seeing any red face which is $\frac 2 3 \cdot \frac{1}{3} = \frac{2}{9}$.
